1
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:13,164
In episode three one set of lists had two stable matchings, so stable alone does not pick a winner.

2
00:00:13,164 --> 00:00:17,214
To see what separates them, here is a slightly bigger example.

3
00:00:17,214 --> 00:00:24,968
There are four jobs, numbered one to four, and four candidates, Ada, Bea, Cleo and Dora.

4
00:00:25,150 --> 00:00:29,208
Each column shows a list, most wanted first as always.

5
00:00:29,208 --> 00:00:35,670
Notice that every job puts Ada at the top, and that the favourite of Ada is job one.

6
00:00:35,850 --> 00:00:42,514
Let us hunt for the stable matchings, starting with job one and Ada, who are first on each other's lists.

7
00:00:42,514 --> 00:00:48,388
If they were not together, each would prefer the other to its partner, and they would be a rogue couple.

8
00:00:48,388 --> 00:00:52,614
So every stable matching pairs job one with Ada.

9
00:00:52,783 --> 00:00:55,801
That already tells us something about job two.

10
00:00:55,801 --> 00:01:02,365
Its first choice is Ada as well, but in a stable matching Ada is always taken by job one.

11
00:01:02,365 --> 00:01:06,755
So the top of a list is not always a realistic hope.

12
00:01:06,916 --> 00:01:09,880
With Ada out of reach, look at job four.

13
00:01:09,880 --> 00:01:14,428
Its next name is Bea, and the favourite of Bea is job four.

14
00:01:14,428 --> 00:01:20,274
Apart, job four would have someone below Bea, and Bea would have a job below her favourite.

15
00:01:20,274 --> 00:01:25,800
So job four and Bea are together in every stable matching as well.

16
00:01:25,983 --> 00:01:32,191
That leaves jobs two and three with Cleo and Dora, and they can be paired in only two ways.

17
00:01:32,191 --> 00:01:38,725
Either job two takes Dora and job three takes Cleo, or the other way round.

18
00:01:38,883 --> 00:01:40,875
Is the first way stable?

19
00:01:40,875 --> 00:01:47,229
Jobs two, three and four each have the second name on their lists, and the only name above it is Ada.

20
00:01:47,229 --> 00:01:52,737
Ada has her favourite and will not move, so there is no rogue couple.

21
00:01:52,916 --> 00:01:58,166
Now the other way round, where job two has Cleo and job three has Dora.

22
00:01:58,166 --> 00:02:03,560
Job two would rather have Dora, and job three would rather have Cleo or Bea.

23
00:02:03,560 --> 00:02:09,030
But Dora, Cleo and Bea each have their favourite job here, so none of them would move.

24
00:02:09,030 --> 00:02:13,432
Both matchings are stable, and they are the only ones.

25
00:02:14,583 --> 00:02:17,913
Now compare the two through the eyes of job two.

26
00:02:17,913 --> 00:02:21,993
In the first matching it has Dora, the second name on its list,

27
00:02:21,993 --> 00:02:25,231
and in the second matching it has Cleo, the third.

28
00:02:25,231 --> 00:02:32,453
Dora is the best partner job two has in any stable matching, and we call her its optimal candidate.

29
00:02:32,616 --> 00:02:34,908
Do the same for the other jobs.

30
00:02:34,908 --> 00:02:41,944
Job one has Ada and job four has Bea in both, and job three is better off with Cleo than with Dora.

31
00:02:41,944 --> 00:02:46,844
So the first matching gives every job its optimal candidate at once,

32
00:02:46,844 --> 00:02:49,870
and such a matching is called job optimal.

33
00:02:50,050 --> 00:02:52,630
Now take the side of the candidates.

34
00:02:52,630 --> 00:02:59,460
Cleo has job three in the first matching and job two in the second, and job two is higher on her list.

35
00:02:59,460 --> 00:03:06,402
Dora has job two in the first and job three in the second, and job three is higher on hers.

36
00:03:06,583 --> 00:03:13,075
So the second matching gives every candidate her best stable job, and it is called candidate optimal.

37
00:03:13,075 --> 00:03:18,803
And the first matching gives Cleo and Dora the lowest job they have in any stable matching.

38
00:03:18,803 --> 00:03:21,585
The word for that is pessimal.

39
00:03:21,750 --> 00:03:26,648
So here the matching that is optimal for the jobs is pessimal for the candidates.

40
00:03:26,648 --> 00:03:30,046
Is that an accident of these lists, or a law?

41
00:03:30,046 --> 00:03:34,094
Can every job always get its optimal candidate at once?

42
00:03:34,094 --> 00:03:38,420
And which of the two does propose and reject produce?

